#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158472 :tutorial/content "The **union** of two sets ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) denoted ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) is the set of all elements belonging to either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\nFor example, consider the sets ![](images/32/3228232fb4c16493d5268b308ede8727a84c20297d9b216a74db7b537011c704.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d576c7a3682975f73724c0fbddaf962449049968b852a28434102128ffa8a96.svg) We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/8351db4817ac8b0d2991fe8ef09ee1f2583c84cb772114ef0614a0ef96c96389.png)\n\nThe union ![](images/58/58a13b2e88b95997772105b179139f2bb67105050519a451c17b838337cb4632.svg) consists of all the elements that are in ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. To represent ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) we will shade in all the area that is contained within either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\n![](images/e9/e912fe53d530ac47245c916bd16f1758df487ded32da88abfab253ed1ee0cc63.png)\n\nThe elements ![](images/0e/0e9479f799610ac47cadcc6890bb35f5336c08c9e2827384fd299138b7e4c558.svg) are in either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. Therefore, the union of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) is\n\n![](images/dc/dc7e001d55359f56770a3cdd382a7318d77cbb12b260141399192d14953b4146.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg) belongs to both ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) but we list it only once in ![](images/bb/bbb285be6a3e138649aa4a514c34823a6b39d094dca3427eb34eb4a326854fcf.svg) In a set, each distinct element is written only once." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d7d339f-bbdd-5e0c-9913-c3e6cdc13958" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4331 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158478 :tutorial/content "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize unions, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a union.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the union $A \\cup B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both.\n\nSo, we will start with the elements of $A,$ and then go through the elements of $B$ to look for any elements that we do not already have. Remember that we are looking for elements that we do *not* already have in $A.$\n\n- For the element $b \\in B,$ we have $b \\in A. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $d \\in B,$ we have $d \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $e \\in B,$ we have $e \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nSo, to get the union, we need to take the elements of $A$ and also include $d$ and $e.$\n\nTherefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA \\cup B = \\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "387bbd7c-3300-588c-9e61-54dda9490e81" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4338 :tutorial/title "Unions Without Venn Diagrams"}]}
